.TH CLOG_SET_SIGPIPE_POLICY 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_set_sigpipe_policy, clog_sigpipe_policy_t \- choose what the logger may do about SIGPIPE
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

typedef enum {
    CLOG_SIGPIPE_AUTO      = 0,   /* the default */
    CLOG_SIGPIPE_UNTOUCHED = 1,
} clog_sigpipe_policy_t;

ccol_retval_t clog_set_sigpipe_policy(clog_sigpipe_policy_t policy);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets, for the whole process, what the library may do about SIGPIPE
when a logger writes to a descriptor whose reader is gone.

A write(2) to a pipe or a socket whose reading end is closed fails
with EPIPE and also raises SIGPIPE, whose default disposition ends the
process.  A logger decides the kind of its descriptor once, with
fstat(2), when it opens (a rotation keeps a regular file), and what
happens then depends on that kind:

Regular file, terminal, or any descriptor that is neither a pipe nor
a socket
    The write cannot raise SIGPIPE, so the library does nothing about
    it under either policy.

Socket
    Under either policy the library writes with send(2) and
    MSG_NOSIGNAL, so the write fails with EPIPE and raises nothing.
    No signal state changes.

Pipe or FIFO, under CLOG_SIGPIPE_AUTO
    The async writer thread of a logger keeps SIGPIPE blocked in its
    own signal mask and takes back the SIGPIPE that its own failed
    write left pending on it, so nothing about the process changes.

    macOS raises the SIGPIPE of a pipe write on the whole process,
    not on the writing thread, so there the async writer thread also
    sets F_SETNOSIGPIPE on the descriptor for the length of each write,
    so that the write fails with EPIPE and raises nothing.  After the
    write, the descriptor gets back the F_SETNOSIGPIPE value that it
    had before.

    Any other thread that writes to a pipe sets the disposition of
    SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN for the whole process, the first time that it
    does so.  Such a write comes from a synchronous logger, from a
    CLOG_FATAL record, or from a record that an async logger writes
    on the calling thread because its queue refused it.  The library
    makes this change at most once, and only when the disposition is
    SIG_DFL at that moment, so a handler or an ignore that the
    application installed stays in place.  Opening a logger changes
    nothing; only the first such write does.

Pipe or FIFO, under CLOG_SIGPIPE_UNTOUCHED
    The library changes no disposition and no signal mask.  The write
    raises SIGPIPE, and the disposition and the signal mask of the
    application decide what happens.

Whatever the policy, a record whose write fails with EPIPE is dropped
silently, like a record that meets any other write error that the
library cannot recover from.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
policy  CLOG_SIGPIPE_AUTO or CLOG_SIGPIPE_UNTOUCHED.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       The policy is in force for every later write.
ccol_invalid_args  policy is not one of the two values above.
                   Nothing changes.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You may call this at any time, from any thread, before or after any
logger exists.  The policy governs every write that starts after the
call, including those of the async writer threads that already run:
under CLOG_SIGPIPE_UNTOUCHED such a thread unblocks SIGPIPE again
before its next write to a pipe, and under CLOG_SIGPIPE_AUTO it blocks
it again.  A writer thread that its creator started with SIGPIPE
already blocked keeps that mask under both policies.

A disposition of SIG_IGN that the library already set under
CLOG_SIGPIPE_AUTO stays in place when the policy changes to
CLOG_SIGPIPE_UNTOUCHED, because the library cannot tell whether that
disposition is still its own, and the application may already rely on
it.  To keep the disposition untouched from the start, call this
before the first write to a pipe.

A disposition of SIG_IGN holds for the whole process, and, as for any
ignored signal, a child that fork(2) and exec(3) start inherits it.
An application that starts children which expect the default
disposition of SIGPIPE either restores it in the child before exec(3),
or chooses CLOG_SIGPIPE_UNTOUCHED and handles SIGPIPE itself.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Thread-safe.  A write that runs at the same time as the call follows
either the old policy or the new one.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* The application handles SIGPIPE itself: it wants no change to the
 * disposition, and it blocks SIGPIPE in the threads that log. */
if (clog_set_sigpipe_policy(CLOG_SIGPIPE_UNTOUCHED) != ccol_success)
    abort();
clog lg = clog_open_fd(STDOUT_FILENO, CLOG_INFO, NULL);
if (lg == CLOG_INVALID)
    abort();
clog_info(lg, "stdout may be a pipe");
clog_close(lg);
.fi
